---
import { datingOverview } from '../../lib/corpus/dating-overview';
const o = await datingOverview();
const fmt = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US');
---
<details class="dating-note" id="how-dates-work">
  <summary>How recordings are dated</summary>
  <p>Every date is an upload or stream date: when a recording was published, not when the events it describes happened. {fmt(o.dated)} of {fmt(o.total)} recordings have a catalog date; the rest show a labelled estimate where one can be made.</p>

  <h4>Catalog dates</h4>
  <p>A date that the platform or the archive displayed for that very recording. There are three exceptions, each someone else's record of the day: 85 recordings carry the date in the file name of an earlier transcript index, one stream the day of the first community post that linked it, and four streams from late 2025 the day a community archivist gave when re-uploading them.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Where the date comes from</th><th scope="col">Recordings</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{o.byBasis.map(([label, n]) => <tr><td>{label}</td><td>{fmt(n)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Checked against independent evidence.</strong> 1,784 catalog dates were compared with archived copies of channel listings or video pages. All agreed to within a day.</li>
    <li><strong>One day of slack.</strong> Older archive files carry the day YouTube displayed (US Pacific time) and newer ones the UTC day, so the same video can appear a day apart in two sources.</li>
    <li><strong>Kick streams.</strong> Their archive file names use a clock ten hours ahead of UTC. Catalog dates are the UTC start day, one day earlier than the file name for about half of them. Archived clip times and the weekdays and clock times said in the streams both support this.</li>
    <li><strong>Re-uploads.</strong> A few recordings re-upload older material. The date is right for the upload; the content is older.</li>
    <li><strong>Dates from an earlier transcript index.</strong> 85 recordings of 2023 and 2024 have no date in any archive. They carry the date in the file name of an earlier transcript index for the same video. Checked against archived video pages, archived listings and community posts, 114 of 115 such dates were within a day of that evidence; they can be a day off. A recording that is a copy of a dated recording keeps that recording's date instead.</li>
    <li><strong>First community link post.</strong> One stream of November 2025 carries the UTC day on which a community post first linked it. For recordings with a known date, such a post fell within a day of that date for 84 of 90.</li>
    <li><strong>Days given by a community archivist.</strong> Four streams of November and December 2025 have no date in any archive. They carry the day the community archivist u/HIMRedits gave when re-uploading them, after the audio of each re-upload was matched to the recording. This is the archivist's statement, not a platform record. Two other re-uploads can be checked against an archive date and differ from it by one day each, so these four dates can be a day off. One was given as one of two days and shows the earlier.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Estimates for recordings without a catalog date</h4>
  <p>Estimates never replace a catalog date. Each one says what it rests on, here and wherever the recording is listed.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Kind of estimate</th><th scope="col">Recordings</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{o.estimates.map(([label, n]) => <tr><td>{label}</td><td>{fmt(n)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Matching text.</strong> The transcript is identical to, or shares most of its text with, a dated recording. Where an archived listing or video page confirms the date, lists show only the dated copy.</li>
    <li><strong>Part of a dated recording.</strong> The words of a clip are found inside a longer dated recording, so the clip is taken to be an excerpt of it and shown with that recording's date as "circa". When 2,500 test snippets were matched with their real source removed, one wrongly reached a firm match. The date is the day of the recording found, not the day the clip was made. Where the recording found may only be a later replay, the date is shown as "or earlier".</li>
    <li><strong>Archived listing.</strong> An archived channel video list showed the video as "N days, weeks or months ago". YouTube rounds such ages down, which gives a window. All 505 videos with a known date fell inside their window when tested.</li>
    <li><strong>Combined evidence.</strong> A range of a week or less from several clues: a community post that quoted or summarised the stream at the time, an archived stream record, a dated excerpt that was cut from the recording, a weekday or date said in the recording, the days left free between a platform's dated streams, or the release date of something mentioned. Shown as "circa". For recordings with a known date, a post that states a stream's day names that day or the day after for 72 of 78, and the first post that quotes a stream falls on its day or the day after for 30 of 36. Where a dated excerpt is the main clue, the range held the true date in 87% of 79 test cases. Where a recording states its own day ("today's the 20th", "election day tomorrow"), the passage was read in context first, because the same words can be a memory or a quotation. Of 26 such statements read on recordings with known dates, 23 were within a day of the catalog date. Twenty statements read on undated recordings could later be checked against the index or a community post; all twenty agreed to within a day.</li>
    <li><strong>Date in title.</strong> The title itself contains a date.</li>
    <li><strong>Estimated period.</strong> An undated clip from an archived collection of Twitch clips gets the period that the collection's dated clips cover.</li>
    <li><strong>Estimated date.</strong> A statistical estimate from the words used, given as a month with a range. On recordings with known dates it lands within three months at the median and within a year for 83%. Tested on 3,039 dated recordings, the stated ranges contained the true date about 81% of the time. For 80 recordings that were dated only after their estimate had been made, the estimate was within a year for 74% and the date found lay inside the stated range for 86%; those estimates ran about half a year early, which the current model corrects in part. For 108 more recordings that other evidence dated in November 2026, the estimate made before was within a year for 96% and the date lay inside the stated range for 94%; those recordings now train the model. No estimate or range goes past 5 December 2025, the day of the last stream of 2025: the model and the combination of clues both treat it as the latest possible day.</li>
    <li><strong>Part of an undated recording.</strong> A clip found inside a longer recording that has no catalog date either is given that recording's estimate, or its circa date where it has one.</li>
    <li><strong>Clips with the same name.</strong> A clip with no match of its own is given the day of another file of the same clip, or the range between its numbered neighbours.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>Limits</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>A release date of a game or phone only gives an earliest date, and only when the recording shows it in use. Mentions alone are not used.</li>
    <li>A weekday said aloud matches the catalog weekday about three times in four, and is within a day more than nine times in ten.</li>
    <li>Dates from the earlier transcript index and from community posts are other people's records of when a stream ran, not platform records. An index date or a first link post is used as a catalog date and the recording's page says so; quotes and summaries in posts give circa dates only.</li>
    <li>For about one recording in twenty the wording estimate is far off: the true date lies more than twice as far from the estimate as the end of the stated range. Other clues are weighed with that in mind, so a label, a listing or a date said aloud can outweigh the wording.</li>
    <li>Recordings with no transcript, or a very short one, usually have no estimate.</li>
  </ul>
</details>
